---
description: React Guidelines
alwaysApply: false
---

- Treat UIs as a thin layer over your data. Skip local state (like `useState`) unless it's absolutely needed
and clearly seperate from business logic. Choose variabes and `useRef` if it doesn't need to be reactive.
- When you find yourself with nested if/else or complex conditional rendering, create a new component.
Reserve inline ternaries for tiny, readable sections.
- Choose to derive data rather than use `useEffect`. Only use `useEffect` when you need to sync with an external system (e.g. DOM).
It causes misdirection of what the logic is doing. Choose to explicitly define logic rather than depend on implicit reactive behavior.
- Treat `setTimeout` as a last resort (and always comment why you use it)
- IMPORTANT: do no add useless comments. avoid adding comments unless you're clarfifying a race condition (setTimeout), a long-term TODO,
or a confusing piece of code even a senior engineer wouldn't initially understand.